          "settings": {
            "question": "What does OEKO-TEX® certified mean?",
            "answer": "<p>OEKO-TEX® STANDARD 100 is an independent certification for textiles. Every component of a certified item is laboratory tested against a long list of substances known to be harmful to human health — not only the main fabric, but the threads, the zip, the foam and any coatings or prints.</p><p>It has four product classes. <strong>Class I is the strictest</strong>: the limits applied to things made for babies and children under three, who chew and suck on what they sleep on. Our pillow is certified at Class I, which is not the usual grade for an adult pillow — most are certified at Class II or III, if at all.</p><p>The certificate is held by the factory that makes the pillow, which is normal: certification follows the production, not the brand name on the box. Certificate <strong>25.HCN.94660</strong>, valid to 31 March 2027 — <a href=\"https://www.oeko-tex.com/en/detail/?number=25.HCN.94660\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">check it on oeko-tex.com</a>.</p>"
